WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.860
The first film tracked one domain to its target.

2
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:07.400
What changes when one protein carries several binding modules?

3
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.740
Those modules can now influence one another's effects.

4
00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.970
Start with cellular Src as an open strip.

5
00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:24.410
It carries an SH3 domain, an SH2 domain, a kinase, and a short tail.

6
00:00:25.770 --> 00:00:27.330
Each block keeps a distinct job.

7
00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:32.250
Flexible connections let those blocks meet within one protein.

8
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:37.610
A kinase called Csk places phosphate on a tail tyrosine.

9
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:40.010
The tail bends back.

10
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.630
Src's own SH2 pocket catches that phosphotyrosine, bringing the tail against the

11
00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.450
protein. This contact uses the reader internally.

12
00:00:51.683 --> 00:00:55.683
The SH3 domain catches the linker at the same time.

13
00:00:56.703 --> 00:01:00.543
These internal contacts settle both domains against the kinase.

14
00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:03.943
Together they stabilize in an inactive shape.

15
00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:07.923
Neither module performs the kinase reaction itself.

16
00:01:09.033 --> 00:01:14.233
Now offer the SH2 domain a better external phosphotyrosine target.

17
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:16.673
Predict what happens.

18
00:01:17.913 --> 00:01:20.533
Can Src's own tail still complete the same grip?

19
00:01:22.153 --> 00:01:24.313
Compare the two targets competing for one pocket.

20
00:01:30.317 --> 00:01:36.337
No. The external target can occupy SH2 and pull it from the tail.

21
00:01:37.837 --> 00:01:40.617
Losing one internal contact loosens the clamp.

22
00:01:41.897 --> 00:01:45.277
Other inputs can also disturb the SH3 contact.

23
00:01:46.657 --> 00:01:49.137
Opening makes kinase activation more accessible.

24
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:56.160
Viral Src has lost the tail and its regulatory tyrosine, along with other mutations.

25
00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:02.740
Without that tail contact, the full internal clamp cannot form, so the kinase is

26
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:07.060
always on. The missing module target removes an off-switch.

27
00:02:08.183 --> 00:02:11.683
The same modular logic can build a conditional detector.

28
00:02:13.083 --> 00:02:20.243
Give one protein an SH2 domain for a phosphorylated receptor and a PH domain for a

29
00:02:20.243 --> 00:02:24.043
membrane lipid. Join them with a flexible linker.

30
00:02:25.033 --> 00:02:29.133
Suppose either contact alone is too weak to retain the detector.

31
00:02:30.433 --> 00:02:33.053
With only the receptor mark, it visits and leaves.

32
00:02:34.353 --> 00:02:36.893
With only the lipid, it also visits and leaves.

33
00:02:38.233 --> 00:02:40.653
One input produces only a brief encounter.

34
00:02:41.733 --> 00:02:45.493
When both targets are close, both domains bind at once.

35
00:02:46.653 --> 00:02:49.113
Each contact helps keep the other within reach.

36
00:02:50.313 --> 00:02:54.053
The detector remains recruited only where the two inputs coincide.

37
00:02:55.553 --> 00:02:57.693
Removing either target again releases it.

38
00:02:58.853 --> 00:03:04.553
This joint retention is avidity: two weak contacts together can behave like one much

39
00:03:04.553 --> 00:03:06.293
tighter interaction at that location.

40
00:03:07.467 --> 00:03:09.067
That is the second rule.

41
00:03:10.407 --> 00:03:14.587
Combining modules can make binding conditional on more than one signal.

42
00:03:15.887 --> 00:03:19.447
In Src, two internal contacts together hold the kinase off.

43
00:03:20.707 --> 00:03:24.967
In the detector, two external contacts together hold the protein in place.
